From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 02:35:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: stmmac: norm_desc: Drop all descriptors of a
 multi-descriptor frame

With normal descriptors, ndesc_get_rx_status() never reports rx_not_ls.
It discards any descriptor without the last-descriptor bit, so frames
larger than one RX buffer are not supported. A frame that overflows into
a second descriptor is only partly dropped, though: its first
descriptor(s) are discarded, but the final descriptor has
RDES0_LAST_DESCRIPTOR set and no error bits, so stmmac_rx() starts a new
skb from it. The tail of the oversized frame is then passed up the stack
as a separate packet, with a bogus Ethernet header and a length taken
from the whole frame.

Discard descriptors that are missing either the first or the last
descriptor bit, so an oversized frame is dropped as a whole and counted
in rx_length.

This shows up on dwmac-sun8i (normal descriptors, 2047 byte buffers)
once the MAC is set to accept frames longer than 1518 bytes.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/norm_desc.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/norm_desc.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/norm_desc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/norm_desc.c
@@ -68,7 +68,13 @@
 	if (unlikely(rdes0 & RDES0_OWN))
 		return dma_own;
 
-	if (unlikely(!(rdes0 & RDES0_LAST_DESCRIPTOR))) {
+	/* Frames spanning more than one descriptor are not supported with
+	 * normal descriptors: drop every descriptor of such a frame. Checking
+	 * only the last descriptor bit would hand the tail of the frame to
+	 * stmmac_rx() as if it were a complete frame.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(!(rdes0 & RDES0_LAST_DESCRIPTOR) ||
+		     !(rdes0 & RDES0_FIRST_DESCRIPTOR))) {
 		x->rx_length++;
 		return discard_frame;
 	}
